speechtext:
I had reference to men who were then attached to different reserve units in England. When I was there I believed, as I believe now, that there was an excess of these men there, and that all were not usefully employed. They were unfit for service in France, according to the classification adopted by the authorities in England. The contention I made was that, if these men were not to be employed usefully in England they should not be left around the camps in England, but should be returned to be engaged in useful work in Canada. There are about 900, I think, according to the list furnished by the Minister of Militia, who are listed for re-
197}
turn to Canada. These men are not necessarily medically unfit. Nor is their acceptance anything against the examination in Canada. In many cases they are men over age, who, on enlisting, perhaps gave their age at much less than it really was. But the winter conditions in England tell very rapidly on old men from this country. They develop rheumatism and other ailments which caused them to be classified as unfit for active service. These are the men to whom I . referred.
